<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Make a Poster! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Make a Poster!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow clear instructions in learning activities. Interactive: Execute multi-step directions in learning activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow clear instructions in learning activities to complete a task (e.g., "First, cut the paper, then glue it on the flowchart."). Can follow and respond to multi-step directions in-class activities (e.g., "You need to fold the paper before you cut it."). Can respond appropriately to oral instructions (e.g., "Let's put away our books.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary related to following instructions for an art activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of children making a poster in a classroom. The teacher asks, "What are they doing?" (Making a poster!). The teacher then shows realia: paper, scissors, and glue. The teacher asks, "What is this?" while showing each item, encouraging students to say the word in English if they know it, or repeating after the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: "paper," "scissors," "glue," "cut," "fold," "paste." The teacher explicitly models the /p/ sound in "paper" and "poster," and the /s/ sound in "scissors" and "paste." The teacher performs simple actions (e.g., "cut the paper," "paste the glue") while saying the imperatives clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visual cues and simple questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows scissors: "Do we eat with scissors?" (No!). "What do we do with scissors?" (Cut!).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows glue: "Is this for drinking?" (No!). "What is this for?" (Paste!/Glue!).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content through an audio recording focusing on following instructions for a simple classroom activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording (or teacher's clear oral delivery) of a short set of instructions for making a simple paper decoration. The recording uses imperatives, sequencing words ("First, next"), and the target vocabulary (paper, fold, cut, glue, colorful). E.g., "First, get a colorful paper. Next, fold the paper in half. Then, cut a small circle. Finally, paste the circle on your card."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand the main points of clear, slow, and carefully articulated instructions on familiar matters related to classroom activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Students listen to the instructions once. Teacher asks, "What are we making?" (A paper decoration / A card).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen a second time. Teacher gives simple statements, and students say "True" or "False."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, cut the paper." (False, it's 'get colorful paper'.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We fold the paper." (True!)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures representing "colorful paper," "fold," "cut," "paste." Students listen a third time and number the pictures in the correct sequence.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on listening to and processing specific details within instructions, using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for plural imperatives ("Let's make a poster!"), sequencing words ("first, next"), and understanding verbs related to art activities (cut, fold, paste, draw). Receptive understanding of future tense ("I will make a mask"). Vocabulary: paper, scissors, glue, pencil, poster, classroom, teacher, project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher reads aloud simple, short instructions with a missing action verb or object. Students listen and complete the sentence orally or by pointing to flashcards.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "First, ____ the paper." (Pause). Students listen and choose "cut" or "fold."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Next, ____ the glue." (Pause). Students listen and choose "paste" or "draw."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Let's make a ____!" (Pause). Students listen and choose "poster" or "mask."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listen and Identify):</b> The teacher provides a series of two-step instructions orally. Students listen and hold up the correct sequence of action flashcards (e.g., "First, cut. Next, paste." – students hold up "cut" card, then "paste" card). This activity reinforces sequencing words and action verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Do):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives a multi-step instruction to the class (e.g., "First, touch your nose. Next, clap your hands."). Students listen carefully and perform the actions. Then, the teacher says, "Let's put away our books!" and students respond appropriately. The teacher emphasizes phonemic awareness: "classroom" (class-room, two syllables) while saying "Let's go to the classroom."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 12-15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students actively listen and follow a set of instructions to begin creating elements for their "Let's Make a Poster!" project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative listening and execution of instructions. Students will listen to the teacher giving multi-step instructions for preparing specific items needed for a "Let's Make a Poster!" project. This could include cutting out shapes, drawing objects, or coloring. The focus is strictly on listening and following directions accurately. Students can ask for clarification if needed: "What do I need to do next?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into small groups. The teacher announces: "Today, we are going to make a poster! Let's get ready!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives the first set of instructions clearly and slowly, using sequencing words and target vocabulary. E.g., "Group 1, first, get the big, colorful paper. Next, fold it in half. Then, cut a small square." (Teacher monitors for listening comprehension and execution).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a second set of instructions for another group or the whole class: "Group 2, first, draw a big pencil on your paper. Next, color it yellow."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and follow the instructions. If a student is unsure, they are encouraged to ask, "What do I need to do next?" or "Can you say that again, teacher?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reinforces future intentions: "Good job! Later, we will use these for our poster." "Who wants to make a mask next time? I will make a mask!"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' ability to follow instructions, along with self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will listen to a final set of instructions from the teacher for organizing their prepared poster elements. E.g., "First, put your cut shapes on the right side of your desk. Next, place your drawn pencils on the left side. Finally, put all your materials in your art box."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students follow multi-step instructions correctly (e.g., place items in the right sequence/location).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students respond appropriately to oral instructions (e.g., put away books).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use appropriate pragmatic expressions to ask for clarification when needed ("What do I need to do next?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students demonstrate comprehension of target vocabulary and imperatives through actions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper or verbally:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I understand all the teacher's instructions today?" (Yes/No/Sometimes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one instruction that was easy to follow?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, and connection to the next lesson in the theme sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I hear and understand today?" (e.g., cut, paste, fold).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to follow the instructions? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote listening comprehension? The use of realia and TPR was effective for concrete instructions. Modeling pronunciation of /p/ and /s/ during warm-up helped.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with multi-step instructions beyond two steps. I need to continue scaffolding by breaking down longer instructions and using more visual aids. More practice with "first, next, then."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the CEFR A1.3 expectations met? Yes, students could follow clear, slow, single and two-step instructions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a short set of written instructions related to making a simple art project, using the same core vocabulary (paper, scissors, glue, cut, fold, paste) introduced today, to deepen their comprehension of the context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>